WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
Good morning John! I really like backstages.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.280
Yesterday... which is today actually

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:11.900
we played - or are about to play, depending on how you look at it - at the... McGlohan Theatre, possibly.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:17.850
It's in Charlotte, North Carolina. It's a beautiful theater. It has a dome, it has, like, red carpet everywhere,

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:23.660
there's stained glass windows... but upon arriving at a venue the thing I want to see first is the backstage.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:29.460
I want to see how weird and different it is and I want to try to understand how it works a little bit

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.140
even though like when you actually get to a backstage you realize the glamour isn't...

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:37.860
isn't really there. Like, you think about backstage as like a place where only a few people get to go.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.500
There's backstage passes, and that has a very specific connotation

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.220
but like it's usually dingy and gross and like there's, you know, like utility sinks.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.120
But every venue is kind of the same from the front.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:52.480
Like, of course it's very different going from like a beautiful room like this

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:53.940
to like a middle school auditorium

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.280
But it's just a stage with a bunch of chairs when it comes down to it.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.240
But the layout of the backstage is always completely different.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.920
Like at the McGlohon we walked down three flights of stairs before we got like to the green room.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.240
And like backstage at

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.060
VidCon I mean it looks - and smells -
 like a high school cafeteria.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.320
There's sort of two reactions you can have to a backstage.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.060
You can get back there and be like oh, this is, you know, the glamour is gone

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.520
I now understand that this is just a room, and indeed kind of a less good room

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.320
than all the front-facing parts of a venue.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:29.600
But then there's the other way to look at it, which is the kind of all the things that make the world actually work

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.880
tend to be hidden from us.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.400
They're above the drop ceiling, they're behind the scenes, under the hood.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.340
All the pipes and wires and servers and ducts, we have this instinct to hide them

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:44.180
so that we can like create a nice shiny glossy beautiful thing

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:48.360
that may - it's like all the things that we actually, there's a lot that goes into

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.880
are hidden so that we can make it look like magic.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:57.580
Being able to see and start to understand the magic, I think that's a big reason why I love backstages.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:02.960
Because they don't have drop ceilings. They're like hey, just we're not gonna pay to make this all fancy.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.100
I'm obsessed with this stuff like if you watch my old tour vlog, from last year,

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:12.260
Oh man, I got myself into some places. I've tried to get myself into some places here... and I failed, unfortunately.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.480
Where's room 2-0-4?

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.800
Oh no... Hank's gotta go in there!

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:23.640
It's locked. No!

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.640
Nothing - just the secret room is locked.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.240
Oh, look at the weird dirt! Oh, that would have been cool.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.240
The other dressing room also has a secret door with a less creepy way to get to it

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.360
But is it also locked? Probably.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.380
Probably... oh, I love little doors!

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.400
Ahhhhhh

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.820
Any door that is not the size of a full-height man is like the fit... like, I don't know what it is...

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.320
It gets me very... intrigued.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:57.660
I think sometimes we're too good at hiding the hard work that goes into making things function.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.360
But like I love beautiful design, I love beautiful things

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:05.100
but I also want that magic to be stripped away a little bit so that we can appreciate

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:09.520
the beauty of the function. Like, a car engine is as beautiful as a car

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:15.740
and CPU is as beautiful as a phone, and a backstage is as beautiful as a stage.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:18.340
John, I'll see you... uh, when I see you.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:23.620
John, it's been really fun to hang out. The tour bus is amazing! Though I have identified a problem with it.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:28.060
Yeah... I don't know how this has happened, but they are.... they're... they're just everywhere.

